    bio: "Born Gerard de Kremer on March 5, 1512, in Rupelmonde in the County of Flanders, Gerardus Mercator became the most influential cartographer in history. Educated at the University of Leuven, where he studied philosophy and theology before turning to mathematics and astronomy, he apprenticed under the renowned instrument maker and cartographer Gemma Frisius. By his late twenties he had established himself as a supremely skilled engraver, globe maker, and mapmaker, producing a celebrated map of Palestine in 1537 and a landmark map of the world in 1538 that first used the name \"America\" for both continents of the Western Hemisphere.\n\nIn 1544, Mercator was arrested and imprisoned for seven months on charges of heresy — his extensive travels for geographic research had aroused suspicion among Catholic authorities in the Spanish Netherlands. After his release, he relocated to Duisburg in the Duchy of Cleves, where he spent the rest of his life in relative safety and extraordinary productivity. In 1569, he published his world map using the revolutionary cylindrical projection that bears his name, which rendered lines of constant compass bearing as straight lines on the map — a breakthrough that transformed oceanic navigation and remains the basis of most standard world maps to this day.\n\nMercator spent his final decades laboring over his greatest ambition: a comprehensive cosmography that would describe the entire creation from its divine origins to the geography of the modern world. He published the first volumes of his atlas — a word he coined for a collection of maps — but died on December 2, 1594, before completing the project. His son Rumold published the full atlas posthumously in 1595, and its influence was immense, shaping how Europeans understood and visualized the globe for centuries to come."
